  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/4a2298a8255a86c803560793658593a22f7f77590ef350c1e0cebd974fce7750.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a sharp edge (corner) at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)$ does not exist. Hence, $x=2$ is a critical point of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, all statements are true.",
